  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\n\nToday and tonight...\n\nUpper level ridging remains in control with a seasonably warm and\ndry airmass beneath northerly flow aloft. High temperatures this\nafternoon will climb into the lower 90s with deep mixing by\nafternoon. With ongoing drought conditions and RH values (%) in \nthe mid-20s, a \"Limited\" fire danger outlook continues to be\nadvertised over northern parts of the area. Continue to be \ncautious when burning outdoors and to heed any local burn bans.\n\nWednesday into early next week...\n\nAs the surface ridge migrates eastward through mid and late week,\nsoutherly return flow will advect deep Gulf moisture into the \narea which will ease fire weather concerns with increasing\nhumidity through the weekend. \n\nBy late week, an upper low will become entangled with the remnants\nof Eastern Pacific Hurricane Polo and the merged system will then\neject into the central parts of the country. At the same time, the\nsubtropical ridge over the southwest Atlantic will strengthen,\nresulting the in the upper low struggling to penetrate into the \nDeep South and instead being shunted into the Midwest. While the \nbulk of this storm complex will miss the area to the north and \nwest, deep moisture associated with a tropical wave that becomes \nentrained into the system from the Atlantic side will combine with\nthe glancing influence of midlatitude cyclone, bringing \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms Friday and into the weekend. \nSevere weather is not currently anticipated with this activity. \nRainfall totals of 2-4 inches appear likely for most over the \nweekend, which would help improve ongoing drought conditions. As \nthe storm complex shifts northeast and away, a trailing cold front\nwill mark the back edge of our primary rain opportunities, moving\nthrough the area early next week. Behind the front, a substantial\ncooldown appears on tap; highs early next week likely fall into\nthe lower 80s F for most with lows dipping into the upper 50s F \nand lower 60s F across the area. /86/SW/",
